ops(OPS-43): land the three long-window defects the 2026-09-09 XL runs paid for

All three were measured today and none was mechanised; §5.1 now states them
as rules and OPS-43 makes them checkable rather than remembered.

1. A long window must survive its client. TH-11 step 5d attempt 1 was killed
   at ~40 min on the wrapper side: no ## Exit footer, so nothing in the log
   was countable, while the container-side timeout, mpiexec and all eight
   ranks kept running at ~85% CPU on 260 GiB with nobody consuming their
   output. The fix is to redirect container-side to a gitignored
   /workspace/logs/<name>-raw.log and echo it back; a bare pipe or tee does
   the opposite, since tee takes SIGPIPE when the client dies and the run
   dies with it. The relaunch uses this and is running.

2. After any killed window, check for orphaned ranks before re-running. A
   dead wrapper does not stop the compute.

3. memory.peak is per container lifetime and read-only on this kernel
   (WSL2 6.6), so a reading after a second run is the maximum over every run
   since the container started. Either restart the service before an xl
   window or have the module print ru_maxrss. ANS-4 step 2b's ledger row
   already carries that caveat.

Plus the rule the mis-tiering came from: no chunk is marked xl without a
measured memory price, and a censored reading (TH-11 step 5's "0.99 M pegged
at memory.max = 64.00 GiB") is not one -- it bounds demand from below and
licenses no extrapolation.

CLAUDE.md carries the two an agent must not rediscover. No band, record or
src/ change; documentation and policy only.

